# Agentarchy desktop packages -- KDE Plasma 6 on Wayland.
#
# Verified in a QEMU/virtio-gpu guest (spike, 2026-08-23): this set boots vanilla Arch to a Plasma
# Wayland session with SDDM autologin in about 3m20s. Same format and consumer as
# install/agentarchy-base.packages; the base list is installed first and is never repeated here.
#
# We deliberately do NOT install plasma-meta. What that group adds and why we leave it out:
#   discover                    - a GUI app store over pacman/flatpak; Agentarchy manages packages
#                                 through oal-* commands, and Discover invites AUR-less confusion
#   plasma-welcome              - a first-run tour branded for stock Plasma
#   plasma-vault                - encrypted folders; overlaps the age-based flow we already ship
#   krdp, plasma-thunderbolt,   - remote desktop / niche hardware; opt-in, not default surface
#   wacomtablet, plasma-disks
#   plasma-firewall             - a GUI for firewalld/ufw; base already installs ufw and oal- owns it
#   oxygen, oxygen-sounds       - the pre-Breeze theme; dead weight for a themed distro
#   plasma-browser-integration  - needs a browser extension to do anything
#   plasma-sdk                  - Plasma development tools, not a desktop need
# Anything above can still be installed by hand; none of it is required to reach a working desktop.
#
# Several entries below are already transitive dependencies of plasma-desktop/plasma-workspace
# (kwin, powerdevil, systemsettings, kde-cli-tools, kconfig, qt6-tools, xorg-xwayland). They stay
# explicit on purpose: oal-* scripts call their binaries directly (kreadconfig6/kwriteconfig6 from
# kconfig, qdbus6 from qt6-tools, plasma-apply-* from plasma-workspace), so a dependency change
# upstream must not be able to silently take a tool we depend on out of the install.

# --- session core
plasma-desktop
plasma-workspace
kwin
kscreen
plasma-integration
qt6-wayland
xorg-xwayland

# --- display manager (autologin straight into the Wayland session; greeter stays X11)
sddm

# --- theming: Breeze is the base every Agentarchy theme is applied on top of
breeze
breeze-gtk
qqc2-breeze-style
kde-gtk-config

# --- hardware and system integration applets
plasma-nm
plasma-pa
powerdevil
bluedevil
print-manager
xdg-desktop-portal-kde
kwallet-pam

# --- tools oal-* drives directly
kconfig
qt6-tools
kde-cli-tools
systemsettings

# --- the menu
# fuzzel backs oal-menu-select: a Wayland-native picker, ~200 KB, themed from the current palette
# like everything else. The alternative was reviving a compositor shell to get a menu back.
fuzzel

# --- desktop applications
dolphin
konsole
ghostty
kate
spectacle
ark
gwenview
okular
plasma-systemmonitor
kdeplasma-addons

# Icon set. The theme engine picks a folder colour from each palette (bin/oal-theme-set-icons),
# which needs Papirus present; without it the desktop falls back to Breeze and says so.
papirus-icon-theme

# Two gaps a desktop notices immediately. applications/mpv.desktop and applications/imv.desktop are
# vendored and were naming software nothing installed, so a video file opened in nothing at all.
mpv
kcalc

# Local inference. This is what makes "agent-first operating system" true with no network, no
# account and no bill -- and it is the clearest evidence the brain contract is not a bet on one
# vendor, since it shares nothing with the others but four subcommands.
#
# The CPU build ships everywhere because it works everywhere. install/agent/local-inference.sh swaps
# in ollama-cuda on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU; a VM gets CPU, because a virtio GPU is for graphics
# and cannot do inference.
#
# No model ships with it. Models are gigabytes and choosing one for somebody is a worse default than
# asking: `ollama pull qwen2.5:1.5b` is small enough for a laptop CPU.
ollama

# The browser. A desktop without one cannot open a link, and until now nothing here could: no
# package named a browser, and xdg-settings had no default to report.
#
# Chromium rather than Firefox because every piece of support around it is already here and inert --
# the theme template, config/chromium-flags.conf, install/user/chromium.sh, and the default-browser
# step in oal-provision-user, which has been printing "skipping ... not installed". It is also what
# browser automation drives, which matters for an agent-first machine.
#
# Brave is one command away and inherits all of it, being the same engine:
#   oal-install-browser brave
# That route also creates /etc/<browser>/policies/managed world-writable so a theme change can tint
# the browser chrome. That is upstream's design and it is a deliberate act, not a default: a
# world-writable policy directory under /etc lets any local process set browser policy, which is too
# much to hand over for a title-bar colour.
chromium

# The menu picker. It has to work with a mouse, which is why the menu is a native Qt dialog rather
# than the prettier keyboard-first picker; see docs/menu.md.
kdialog
